course: dictionary
lesson: 12948
title: Exceptionally
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Exceptionally comes from the word exception, which is something that does not follow the usual pattern. Most often it means very, or much more than usual: «Maya is exceptionally clever at maths.» The clue is clever at maths. You can tell it means very clever, far cleverer than most children.
explain: Exceptionally can also mean in an unusual way, as a one-off. «Exceptionally, the library opened on Sunday.» The word shows that opening on Sunday was a special case, not the normal rule. Ask yourself: is this normal, or is it an exception?
explain: Look at the words near it. In a weather report, «It will be exceptionally cold tonight» means colder than usual for the time of year. Good, hot, cold and well are words that often come after it, and they tell you which meaning fits.
fact: Exceptionally is an adverb, so it tells you how much or how something is done.
fact: «Exceptionally» means very, or much more than usual, as in «exceptionally good».
fact: «Exceptionally» can also mean in an unusual way, as an exception to the rule.
fact: Phrases you will meet include «exceptionally well» and «exceptionally hot».
fact: A quick check is to swap in very or much more than usual, and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «Her painting was exceptionally good for her age.» What does exceptionally mean here? | Very slowly | *Very, much better than usual | Under the table | Late at night
exercise: «Exceptionally, the bus ran on a holiday.» What does exceptionally show? | *This was a one-off, not the usual rule | The bus was empty | The bus was broken | The bus was very fast
exercise: The weather report says «exceptionally hot for April». What does it mean? | Hot in the usual way for April | The sun was hidden all day | *Hotter than usual for April | Cold and wet
test: Which word can swap in for exceptionally in «exceptionally kind»? | Quietly | Slightly | Rarely | *Very
test: Mrs Hall says, «You have worked exceptionally well this term.» What is she praising? | Your handwriting only | Your shoes | Your lunch box | *Your hard work, which is better than usual
exam: Ravi says exceptionally is always the same as very. Is he right? | Yes, it always means very | *No, it can also mean in an unusual way, as an exception | No, it means very bad | Yes, but only in weather reports
